  "credits": {
    "author": "Paul W. Snyder •Conjunctival biopsy—conjunctivitis.",
    "consulting_editor": "Melinda S. Camus TREATMENT •Directed at controlling keratoconjunctivitis sicca. •Any concurrent disease must be medically managed. •May include administration of antiinflammatory or immunosuppressive drugs. •Surgical management of keratoconjunctivitis sicca indicated in animals that fail to respond to medical treatment. MEDICATIONS DRUG(S) OF CHOICE •Topical tear preparations. •Appropriate topical antibiotics for secondary bacterial infection, if present. •Immunosuppressive or anti-inflammatory drugs. •For more aggressive medical treatment and surgical intervention, see Keratoconjunctivitis Sicca. CONTRAINDICATIONS/POSSIBLE INTERACTIONS Use of topical steroids in patients with acute keratoconjunctivitis sicca may cause corneal ulceration and is not recommended. FOLLOW-UP •Reexamine patients weekly until keratoconjunctivitis sicca controlled. •Additional monitoring may be indicated to manage underlying or concurrent disease. •Immunosuppressive drugs—monitor patients every other week for possible side effects. •Prognosis variable and depends on existence of concurrent disease. MISCELLANEOUS A disorder of dogs resembling Sjogren’s syndrome. Clin Immunol Immunopathol 1979, 12:471–476.",
    "acknowledgment": null
  },
